PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core legal rule (Ontario SGA s.19 Rule 2): for specific goods requiring seller work to reach deliverable state, property/risk passes only after work is completed AND buyer receives notice that it has been done. June 2 facts undisputedly leave MacBook non-deliverable; risk stays with Jake. Flood occurs after June 5 text ("almost finished…ready tomorrow") but before June 6 pickup. 

Disagreement centers on whether June 5 message satisfies the "notice that it has been done" element: B group treats text + later narrative statement that Jake "replaced the screen" as adequate completion + notice, so risk passed; C group (only 5 & 8) insists "almost finished" wording is not notice of completed work, so risk never passed and refund required. A/D/E ruled out by all because insurance irrelevant, possession not required, and goods plainly not deliverable on June 2. No other statutory provisions or facts cited. 

My current view leans C on literal notice timing, but B majority and narrative tense create unresolved ambiguity needing clarification on exact completion moment.}

